"""Human-authored teaching material. Numbers in {fields} bind to frozen evidence."""
PAGES=[]
def page(title,topic,body,takeaway,check,figure=None,table=None,sources='Implementation docs and frozen campaign definitions'):
 PAGES.append(dict(title=title,topic=topic,body=body,takeaway=takeaway,check=check,figure=figure,table=table,sources=sources))
page('What we built, learned, and still need to prove','START HERE',[
 ('The question','Can the target receive a useful completed prefix sooner, and can the drafter spend less effort on unlikely outcomes, so the whole response finishes sooner? Our engineering target in the proposal was a 10% latency reduction over the strongest matched baseline. That target has not been achieved.'),
 ('What exists','We have working AR, synchronous SD and complete-cache SSD on L40S; a shared numerical reference; safe uniform, bounded and overlapping publication; and a static unequal-depth prototype. Reproducible drivers freeze prompts, tune settings, check outputs and monitor dedicated GPU use.'),
 ('What the measurements say','The completed shared baseline has mean fixed-output latencies of {ar_fixed:.3f} s for AR, {sd_fixed:.3f} s for SD, and {ssd_fixed:.3f} s for SSD. These gains come from established speculation methods. Our publication variants are about 1% slower than tuned complete K=7 SSD. Unequal-depth findings are provisional and also slower.'),
 ('How to use this guide','Read the route on page 2, then work through the indicated pages. Every chart has a source and an interpretation. The speaker guide supplies the timed talk. Pages 41–45 contain questions with answers. A measured negative result is useful when its controls explain why the attractive idea did not pay off.')],
 'Say what was built, then what evidence establishes. Never call an unmeasured optimization a speedup.',
 'Can you distinguish the SSD baseline gain from our project’s incremental contribution?',sources='baseline; publication; analysis.json; depth_status; greedy_validation')
page('Three routes through the same evidence','STUDY ROUTES',[
 ('Two hours: presentation essentials','Overview 15m (pp. 1, 3, 7); setup 20m (pp. 9–15, skim); profiling 25m (pp. 16–22); results 25m (pp. 26–28, 32–36); correctness and limits 15m (pp. 23–25, 39); rehearsal 20m (main slides and pp. 41–45). Read the takeaway and answer the self-check on every selected page.'),
 ('Half day: understand the evidence','System 25m (pp. 3–8); setup 30m (pp. 9–15); profiling 40m (pp. 16–22); results 45m (pp. 26–36); correctness and implementation 35m (pp. 6, 23–25, 29–31, 35, 37); rehearsal 45m; breaks 20m. Total four hours. Explain two plots aloud without reading their captions.'),
 ('Full day: defend the implementation','System 30m (pp. 3–8); setup 45m (pp. 9–15); profiling 80m (pp. 16–22 plus backup slides); results 90m (pp. 26–36 plus figure_values.csv); correctness 55m (pp. 23–25 and raw checks); implementation 55m (pp. 6, 29–31, 35, 37); remaining plan 25m (pp. 38–40); rehearsal 55m; breaks 45m. Total eight hours.'),
 ('Completion test','Deliver the eleven main slides in ten minutes. Explain the K10-versus-K7 comparison, why 28.6% fewer rows did not yield a speedup, why NCCL activity is not transfer time, and which AR reference passed. If any explanation is uncertain, revisit its worked example rather than memorizing a number.')],
 'The routes change depth and practice time; they do not change the conclusions.',
 'Choose a route now and mark the three questions you find hardest.')
page('Follow one request through the system','SYSTEM MAP',[
 ('Prefill comes first','The target reads the full input and creates key/value attention state, called the KV cache. SSD also prepares the draft model’s state. Reusing model weights or compiled kernels is allowed; skipping the scored prompt’s full prefill is not. Input processing and output generation have different costs.'),
 ('AR and synchronous SD','AR asks the target for one next token at a time. In synchronous SD, the small model first proposes several tokens and the target then verifies them together. Rejected guesses never become committed output. A correction or bonus token lets generation advance even when draft guesses end.'),
 ('SSD overlaps different jobs','While the target verifies the current proposal, the separate drafter prepares continuations for plausible next outcomes. An outcome encodes the accepted-prefix position and a recovery-token guess. A matching cached continuation saves just-in-time drafting; a miss invokes the declared JIT fallback.'),
 ('Our insertion point','We change preparation and handoff of these continuations. A shorter completed continuation can be safe to verify. It is not necessarily faster overall: more short rounds, copies, communication, and final draining can erase the earlier handoff. The target remains responsible for every committed token.')],
 'Optimize time until committed output is ready, not the drafter’s isolated completion time.',
 'Point to where a cache miss and a partial-prefix response enter the request.',figure='architecture',sources='workload; docs/prefix_publication.md')
page('What our GPU setup means','HARDWARE',[
 ('Two L40S devices','Each GPU has its own local memory. The target is Qwen3-8B and the drafter is Qwen3-0.6B. AR uses GPU0; SD puts both models on GPU0; SSD puts the target on GPU0 and drafter on GPU1. Comparing response latency therefore gives SSD an additional GPU. We do not claim equal-resource throughput efficiency.'),
 ('PCIe, NUMA and no NVLink','PCIe connects devices through the host system. The measured topology is SYS: the GPUs are associated with different CPU NUMA sockets, and communication traverses the host interconnect. NUMA means access cost depends on which CPU/memory domain is used. No NVLink means there is no direct NVLink fabric between these GPUs.'),
 ('BF16, capacity and memory allocation','BF16 is a compact floating-point representation. It saves memory and supports fast arithmetic, but rounding can affect close numerical choices. Model capacity is 4,096 tokens; scored initial prompts fit completely within 2,048. Performance workers request 0.70 KV-memory fractions for each model. That parameter is not a claim that exactly 70% of all memory is useful live token data.'),
 ('Scope of our conclusion','These are warm, greedy, single-request experiments on this pair of L40S GPUs. Different interconnects, model sizes or multi-request serving can change the balance. CUDA graphs reduce repeated CPU launch overhead but do not eliminate model computation, synchronization or communication.')],
 'State GPU counts next to latency numbers; topology affects the execution you measured.',
 'Would 2.8× faster SSD on two GPUs establish 2.8× better hardware efficiency?',sources='workload hardware/spec; docs/perf_analysis.md')
page('Submitted work is not completed work','CPU AND GPU EXECUTION',[
 ('The CPU submits commands','Python and the engine prepare tensors, choose graph replays and queue GPU operations. The CPU can return from submission while the GPU is still executing those commands. “The next draft step was submitted” means it was placed in a queue; it does not mean its token or KV writes are readable.'),
 ('A stream orders work','A CUDA stream preserves the ordering of its commands. Independent streams can create opportunities for concurrency when dependencies and GPU resources allow it. A separate stream is not proof that operations actually overlapped. An event recorded after a step provides evidence that the step’s preceding writes are complete.'),
 ('Waiting and querying are different','A blocking wait stops the host until completion. A nonblocking event query checks readiness and lets the host do something else if the event is incomplete. Removing every wait would be unsafe. We still need backpressure so the host does not queue arbitrarily far ahead, and ownership barriers before memory reuse.'),
 ('P4 and queue bound two','With K=7, P=4, the engine is eligible to publish after at least four draft tokens are ready. The bound of two limits unfinished submitted draft steps; it is not two user requests or two outcome branches. A target request can arrive while a later submitted step still runs. The two publication paths differ in whether that tail must finish before delivery.')],
 'Submission is a promise of future execution. Completion authorizes reading the result.',
 'Four tokens are complete and step five is running: what may the consumer read?',sources='docs/adaptive_publication.md; docs/prefix_publication.md')
page('Why safe handoff needs ownership','KV STATE AND SAFETY',[
 ('Tokens have a history','A token prediction and its KV state are valid for a particular committed history. If the target rejects a proposal or advances differently, older speculative state may no longer match. Reading a completed buffer from the wrong generation is still incorrect.'),
 ('Our checks','Requests and cached state carry generation/epoch and expected-context information. Each outcome has a valid length. Completion events protect writes. Only an eligible completed prefix is exposed; uncomputed columns and invalid parent positions cannot be accepted. The target verifies the declared prefix before output is committed.'),
 ('Why snapshots help','The overlap implementation copies eligible completed tokens and score columns to independent preallocated buffers. The consumer can use that snapshot while the producer’s already-submitted tail writes elsewhere. The snapshot cannot be reused until its consumer finishes. Canonical model and KV buffers cannot be reused until old readers and submitted writers finish.'),
 ('Drain is part of the cost','After a target decision, some queued draft work is obsolete. A running kernel cannot simply be erased. Stop new obsolete launches and wait for outstanding accesses before reuse. The full-request timer includes the final drain. Hiding this cleanup after the timer would make the comparison artificially favorable.')],
 'Completion, matching history and exclusive buffer lifetime are three separate requirements.',
 'Why is “the first four tokens are correct” insufficient to justify reusing the producer buffer?',sources='docs/prefix_publication.md; docs/greedy_handoff_queue.md')
page('Decode our configuration names','EXPERIMENT VOCABULARY',[
 ('The names are experimental knobs','A method is an algorithm family: AR, SD, complete SSD or a publication variant. A configuration fixes its parameters. A repetition reruns a setting to estimate run variation. A protocol specifies the stopping rule. A block is the monitored worker unit that becomes valid or invalid together.'),
 ('K, F and P','K is the maximum prepared continuation depth in the settings discussed here. F is the number of recovery-token guesses per possible acceptance position, not a setting for each neural-network layer. At K7/F3, the tree step can process (7+1)×3=24 outcome rows. P is a minimum eligible publication depth. P4 can deliver five or six ready tokens; it is not a demand for exactly four.'),
 ('Queue bound and internal rows','W2 permits at most two unfinished submitted draft steps. The target still computes a captured K+1 verification graph even when fewer draft tokens are valid. Our shared numerical implementation pads some target operations to sixteen rows. None of these row counts changes the request batch size: there is still one user request at a time.')],
 'A configuration is a complete experimental setting; a protocol is how its output stops.',
 'Explain overlap_k7_p4_w2 without using the words “layer batch size”.',table=[['Term','Concrete example'],['Method','Overlap SSD'],['Configuration','K7, F3, P4, W2'],['Repetition','Round 0 through round 4'],['Protocol','Fixed256 or natural EOS/cap256'],['Block','One configuration × round × protocol']],sources='workload/spec; publication queue; outcome-depth queue')
page('Count draft work without claiming speed','WORKED EXAMPLE',[
 ('A complete K7/F3 tree','There are eight possible acceptance positions and three recovery ranks per position: 24 outcomes. Seven draft steps over all 24 rows use 168 query rows. This count concerns the repeated tree forwards; glue, communication and other request work are additional.'),
 ('Unequal depths [7,4,4]','All three ranks get four tokens. Only the first rank continues to seven. The row count is 8×(7+4+4)=120, or 28.6% fewer than 168. The reversed [4,4,7] control has the same count but favors the lower-ranked recovery guess. Uniform [5,5,5] also has 120 rows.'),
 ('Why the equal-work control matters','If ranked depth beats reversed priority, ranking information may be useful. If it fails to beat uniform equal-work depth, its extra allocation complexity is not justified by that comparison. Equal query rows are only a budget control: they do not guarantee equal launch counts, memory traffic, padding, acceptance or elapsed time.'),
 ('K10 is analogous','With eleven acceptance positions, [10,7,4] uses 11×21=231 rows versus 330 for [10,10,10]: 30% fewer. Static ranks are a prototype, not the proposal’s learned scheduler based on full-outcome frequencies and measured cost.')],
 '28.6% fewer model rows is an execution fact, not a predicted 28.6% latency reduction.',
 'Compute the row budget of [4,4,7], and explain what its comparison isolates.',figure='depth_allocation',sources='.worktrees/outcome-depth-v1/docs/outcome_depth.md')
page('Where the real prompts come from','DATASETS',[
 ('Chat is a balanced family, not one topic','We use first-turn prompts from the local public Spec-Bench question file in writing, roleplay, reasoning, math, extraction, STEM and humanities categories. The label “chat” describes a general conversational family. It does not mean every prompt has a long multi-turn conversation.'),
 ('Code and document tasks','Code comes from HumanEval function-completion prompts, wrapped with an instruction to complete the Python function. Document-grounded prompts come from Spec-Bench summarization and retrieval-augmented-generation categories. Their supplied text is part of the input. We measure decoding behavior and latency; we have not reported HumanEval pass@1, factuality or summarization quality.'),
 ('What balanced means','The scored mix contains eight tuning prompts and twenty-five test prompts from each of the three families: 24 tuning and 75 test prompts. A separate warmup prompt per family is reserved. Equal family counts keep a larger source family from dominating the overall mean. The mix is small and does not represent all production traffic.'),
 ('Source traceability','The frozen manifest retains source IDs, category, original text, exact input token IDs and hashes. Three previously diagnosed prompts—specbench-148, HumanEval/36 and specbench-553—are excluded from the declared scored campaign. Later policy studies reuse the selected test prompts; they are not newly unseen data.')],
 'Balanced means equal family counts. It does not mean equally difficult prompts or equal latency.',
 'Why can our “code is faster” finding not establish better generated-code quality?',sources='workload; profiling/workloads.py')
page('What frozen token IDs guarantee','INPUT REPRODUCIBILITY',[
 ('Text becomes numbers','A tokenizer maps a formatted prompt into numerical IDs. Role markers, the generation prompt and thinking-mode settings also affect the sequence. The Qwen3 chat template is applied with thinking disabled, then the exact resulting IDs are saved. These numbers, rather than a freshly reformatted version of the text, enter each method.'),
 ('Why this matters','If AR sees one role marker and SSD sees another, timing and output differences could come from different tasks. Freezing IDs removes that confound. For example, the same visible sentence can tokenize differently after adding a prefix or a different template. This is a conceptual example; our actual complete sequences are in the manifest.'),
 ('Hashes detect drift','A hash is a content fingerprint, not a compressed prompt or proof of semantic correctness. We hash IDs, template, model/tokenizer files and execution sources. A changed input or executor requires an explicit new version; silently resuming would combine incompatible experiments.'),
 ('Length is a different constraint','All chosen scored prompts remain complete within 2,048 initial tokens. We do not deliberately cut off their beginnings to meet the declared campaign limit. The 4,096-token model capacity leaves space for output and internal execution. A later 8K-context study would need a changed capacity, memory checks and a new campaign.')],
 'Frozen IDs ensure the methods receive identical numerical inputs, not identical outputs by themselves.',
 'Name one formatting change that freezing text alone would fail to catch.',sources='workload tokenizer/spec; baseline_common.py manifest validation')
page('Tune first, then freeze the winners','SELECTION',[
 ('The declared baseline grid','AR is the control. SD tries K4, K6 and K8. SSD tries K4, K7 and K10 with F1 and F3. Each setting gets 24 tuning prompts × two repetitions = 48 scored requests. Ten settings in total yield 480 tuning requests. Tune with exactly 256 outputs and EOS ignored.'),
 ('Selection rule','Choose the lowest balanced mean full-request latency on tuning data. Exact ties use smaller K and then F. The baseline selected SD K6 and SSD K7/F3. Natural-EOS results evaluate these same choices; they are not a second opportunity to select a winner.'),
 ('What held-out means here','The baseline’s 75 test prompts are disjoint from tuning and warmup. Five repetitions estimate performance and repeatability. Later policies have repeatedly been evaluated on the same frozen test set. They still have tuning/test separation, but additional unseen data is needed before making strong generalization claims.'),
 ('Read the curve','K10 costs more time in this setup despite offering deeper continuations. Lowering K is a strong simple alternative to an advanced scheduler. That is why our project must compare with tuned complete K7, not just the same oversized K10 configuration.')],
 'A policy is useful only after it beats a strong configuration chosen without test-result tuning.',
 'Why is choosing P after seeing test latency a form of leakage?',figure='baseline_tuning',sources='baseline_selection; workload')
page('Blocks, repetitions and interruptions','RUN ORGANIZATION',[
 ('A block has a fresh worker','A configuration/repetition/protocol block loads the models, compiles and warms the executor, then scores its prompt list. Three separate warmup requests are not scored. Fresh workers limit cross-configuration state reuse. A tuning block contains 24 scored requests; a test block contains 75.'),
 ('Why five rounds','Repeated measurements expose run-to-run variation and check whether outputs repeat. The prompt order is shared across methods within a round. Seeded method orders and alternating protocol order reduce systematic ordering bias. They do not eliminate every temporal effect.'),
 ('What the monitor does','Workers are assigned GPU UUIDs. Before performance blocks, both GPUs must be free of unrelated compute and pass the idle checks. The monitor records external processes during execution. A contaminated or interrupted block cannot count as a completed repetition. No unrelated job is killed.'),
 ('What survives an interruption','Completed valid blocks remain usable. Only the interrupted block is retried when the dedicated window returns. This is block-level resume, not continuation from token 136 inside a timed request. At our snapshot the unequal-depth study has {depth_blocks}/60 valid test blocks; the greedy successor is waiting behind it.')],
 'A waiting queue is not lost work, and an output file without a valid receipt is not a valid block.',
 'If a foreign job arrives after 50 of 75 scored requests, which completed work is retained?',sources='depth_status; greedy_status; block_audit.json')
page('What our latency timer includes','TIMING PROTOCOL',[
 ('Primary interval','The timer starts at request submission and ends when the committed output is ready, including the target and draft prefills and the required final draining of obsolete work. This is full-request latency. Model loading, compilation, warmup and result serialization are outside the interval.'),
 ('Keep measurement quiet','The delivery callback suppresses internal timing-region console output and records first delivery. Ordinary benchmark workers do not attach Nsight capture. Profiling can add overhead or replay kernels, so its durations are used to explain execution, not substituted into the headline timing tables.'),
 ('Warm does not mean cached input','Weights and compiled graphs remain warm. Prefix-cache lookup is disabled between scored requests, forcing complete prompt prefills. Otherwise repeated prompts could become unusually cheap simply because a previous method populated a cache.'),
 ('Fixed versus natural output','Fixed256 ignores EOS and requests the same number of output tokens; it is useful for a controlled work comparison. Natural EOS stops when the model ends, capped at 256. It reflects variable response lengths. We report lengths and agreement so a faster natural run is not mistaken for speed when it merely ended sooner.'),
 ('Overlapping clocks','Do not add target GPU time and draft GPU time to obtain request time. They can run concurrently. A CPU launch range also need not equal the device’s execution span. Compare intervals with the same start/end definition.')],
 'Full-request, decode-only, first-delivery and kernel times answer different questions.',
 'Would stopping the timer before tail draining fairly measure our publication policy?',sources='publication timing contract; workload/spec; profiling/run_engine.py')
page('Read the metrics without mixing them','METRICS',[
 ('Means and percentiles','Mean is the average latency. p50 is the median request; p95 is a high-tail percentile, around the value below which 95% of measured requests fall. A p95 across a mixed prompt set reflects prompt difficulty as well as runtime noise. It is not a confidence bound on the mean.'),
 ('Throughput and first delivery','Committed tokens/s here is total committed tokens divided by total request seconds. It is not an average of each request’s token rate and is not multi-user server throughput. First-delivery latency is time until the first callback output becomes available. It includes prefill and initialization within the request, and may deliver a chunk rather than one displayed word.'),
 ('Acceptance and cache hits','Draft acceptance counts accepted draft tokens divided by verified draft tokens, excluding recovery tokens. Accepted tokens per verification describes useful output per target round. Some “tokens per step” counters include different bookkeeping; use the named verification metric for that comparison. An SSD outcome-cache hit is different from prompt-prefix reuse, which is disabled.'),
 ('Memory and drain','Peak allocated/reserved bytes measure runtime memory usage and reservation, not just useful token state. Our unequal-depth v1 does not reduce reserved KV or rectangular score/snapshot storage. Final-drain time is only one barrier; total request time remains the primary objective.')],
 'Always attach a denominator, interval and unit to a metric.',
 'Why can first delivery get worse while total generation becomes faster?',sources='baseline statistics; publication statistics; analyze_baselines.py')
page('Understand the confidence intervals','STATISTICS',[
 ('Pair the same task','For each method, organize latency as rounds × prompt IDs. A fair comparison draws the same prompts and rounds for method and reference. Resample prompt IDs within chat, code and document families, keeping their balance, and resample repetition rounds. Our saved analyzer performs 10,000 seeded draws.'),
 ('What is calculated','For every draw, calculate the ratio of reference mean latency to method mean latency. The 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles form the reported 95% interval. Speed ratio above one favors the method. A latency-change plot uses the opposite direction: positive change means slower. Check the axis before interpreting an error bar.'),
 ('A small worked example','If the reference averages 2.20 s and a policy averages 2.22 s, the ratio is 2.20/2.22 ≈ 0.991, or roughly 0.9% more latency. An interval entirely below one supports a measured slowdown on this design. It does not prove the policy is slower on every possible workload or GPU.'),
 ('Limits that matter','Five repeats of 75 prompts give 375 measurements, but only 75 distinct prompts. The context diagnostic has one template per family, so its bootstrap does not estimate broad prompt generalization. Historical AR timing comparisons pair prompts but resample old/new rounds independently; they cannot control time-dependent GPU state. Our partial unequal-depth plot uses only complete paired rounds.')],
 'Confidence intervals quantify measured variation within a design; they do not cure design limitations.',
 'Why would treating all 375 requests as unrelated samples exaggerate the available evidence?',sources='saved analyze_baselines.py paired_bootstrap; publication bootstrap contract; analysis.json')
page('How we profiled the project','PROFILING WORKFLOW',[
 ('Start with a machine model','We measured empirical BF16 matrix throughput and memory bandwidth on L40S rather than relying only on datasheet peaks. These measurements define a roofline reference. Then we inspected the algorithm timeline to find the waiting and busy regions that deserve deeper analysis.'),
 ('Nsight Systems campaign','Historical traces cover AR, synchronous SD K6 and SSD K7/F3 on one chat, one code and one document prompt, each producing 256 tokens after three warmup requests. NVTX labels identify prefill, speculate, verify, tree building and request service. CPU activity, CUDA launches, kernels, streams, copies and communication appear on the timeline.'),
 ('Nsight Compute captures','We selected five individual phases: target AR decode, target verify, synchronous draft forward, SSD tree step, and a 631-token target prefill. Hardware counters describe the selected kernels: arithmetic, DRAM traffic, memory/computation utilization, occupancy and stall reasons. These are diagnostic captures, not a repeated latency campaign.'),
 ('Newer measurements','The completed context sweep separately profiles K7 complete, K10 complete and K10 overlap at four input lengths. We correlate tree and verify device spans and ready depths. These profiles describe the shared numerical executor. Historical kernel classifications are labeled with their original executor and are not asserted as a fresh counter measurement of the new one.')],
 'Systems locates the bottleneck in time; Compute helps explain what the selected kernels are limited by.',
 'Which tool would answer “why did GPU0 wait for the drafter” and which would inspect DRAM utilization?',sources='docs/perf_analysis.md; context profiles')
page('Read a timeline from left to right','NSIGHT SYSTEMS',[
 ('Rows are resources and activities','Time runs horizontally. CPU ranges describe host execution and submission. GPU stream rows show when kernels actually execute. A gap means no activity on that displayed row, not automatically that the entire GPU is idle. Inspect all relevant streams and devices.'),
 ('Connect events to the algorithm','First locate target verification. Then find the drafter’s tree steps that overlap it. Identify when the target asks for the next continuation and when the receive completes. A request can hit the outcome cache but still wait for unfinished construction or delivery.'),
 ('Three intervals are easily confused','A CPU call can enqueue kernels quickly and return. A GPU span from first to last kernel can contain launch gaps. A sum of kernel durations excludes those gaps and can double-count wall time when kernels overlap. Our context report names device spans explicitly.'),
 ('Practical exercise','Open the frozen historical wait figure and current phase-balance figure. State their executor, sample size, unit and whether they are profiled. Explain why a long receive kernel does not by itself show the byte-transfer duration. Only a properly correlated dependency trace can identify when each prerequisite became ready.')],
 'Use the timeline to reconstruct dependencies before attributing a long interval to one cause.',
 'Can a short CPU “tree_step” range have GPU work finishing much later?',figure='measured_timeline',sources='context profiles; docs/perf_analysis.md')
page('92.4% busy does not mean 92.4% useful','GPU ACTIVITY',[
 ('The target window','In the saved SSD Systems trace, GPU0 is kernel-busy for about 92.4% of the window. NCCL kernels occupy about 35.2% of the full window. Subtracting that category leaves roughly 57.2% non-NCCL kernel activity. The remaining 7.6% has no active kernel in the measured coverage.'),
 ('What NCCL is doing','NCCL carries GPU communication. A receive kernel may stay active while peer data is not ready, so high measured activity can include waiting. Calling all of its duration memory transfer would confuse synchronization latency with actual link transport. Non-NCCL activity is also only a category; it is not a formal measure of useful FLOPs.'),
 ('The drafter gaps','The drafter GPU is about 72.8% busy in that historical window. Host planning, Python bookkeeping, synchronization, communication dependencies and gaps between short kernels can leave no kernel running. A GPU that is idle between small operations can still be the critical path if it cannot deliver the next continuation quickly enough.'),
 ('Our project implication','The target’s spare useful-execution capacity motivates earlier handoff. However, the important metric is reduced response latency. Adding more small events, copies or short verification rounds can use those gaps without improving the total request.')],
 '“Busy” describes observed execution activity. It is not a guarantee that inference is progressing.',
 'Explain the difference between the 35.2% NCCL share and the 27.8% cache-miss rate.',figure='gpu_states',sources='old_ssd')
page('The cache-hit wait and the dotted line','CRITICAL PATH',[
 ('What was matched','The historical critical-path reconstruction contains 230 draft/verify rounds: 166 hits and 64 misses. Mean target handoff wait is 9.2 ms on hits and 30.0 ms on misses. The dashed horizontal line is mean target verification time, 24.9 ms, included only as a comparison scale.'),
 ('What a hit means','The correct continuation outcome was in the cache’s construction path. It does not mean all its steps had completed by request time. The ready-depth histogram reports depth five for 155 of the 166 hit rounds. A miss invokes JIT drafting, so its mean wait is longer.'),
 ('What we can and cannot separate','The matched trace attributes about 8.0 ms of mean hit wait to the interval until the old tree finished. The remaining difference is about 1.2 ms, but it is not an isolated transfer benchmark: host handling, cache lookup, synchronization and delivery may contribute or overlap. These means cannot establish the exact wire-transfer share.'),
 ('Wait share','Total waiting is about 3.45 s versus 5.72 s verifying. Waiting/(waiting+verification) ≈ 37.6%. That denominator excludes other request work and is not the whole end-to-end stall fraction. The new greedy diagnostic is designed to time prefix readiness, request posting/observation, receipt and full completion separately; it has not run at this snapshot.')],
 'A cache hit can still be late. The dotted line is a duration reference, not a correctness threshold.',
 'Why is 9.2 − 8.0 = 1.2 ms not evidence for exactly 1.2 ms of PCIe transfer?',figure='historical_wait',sources='critical_path; greedy_status; docs/greedy_handoff_queue.md')
page('Read our measured roofline','NSIGHT COMPUTE',[
 ('Two quantities define the roof','Arithmetic intensity is operations divided by bytes transferred from DRAM. The sloped envelope is bandwidth × intensity; the flat roof is measured compute throughput. Low-intensity work can be limited by moving weights even when it uses tensor-core GEMM kernels.'),
 ('Our empirical reference','The machine measurements are about 211 TFLOP/s BF16 tensor throughput and 747 GB/s DRAM-read bandwidth. Their ratio gives a ridge near 283 FLOP per byte. Our historical aggregate verify point is around 13.5 FLOP/B, far to the left; the 631-token prefill is around 365 FLOP/B and is compute-oriented.'),
 ('Why verifying several tokens can be cheap','The target reuses streamed weights across several query rows. At these small widths the same large weight movement dominates, so verification can cost about as much as one-token decode while committing more tokens. This is a measured small-width observation, not a promise that arbitrary K is free.'),
 ('Interpret with care','The plotted whole-forward aggregates include non-GEMM kernels. Hardware counters can count padded arithmetic; some operations benefit from caches, and isolated replay differs from ordinary requests. A point’s exact position is not a universal physical law. This roofline belongs to the historical executor; newer phase-span evidence is separate.')],
 'A GEMM can be memory-bound. More mathematical work does not automatically make verification compute-bound.',
 'Use the sloped roof to explain why 8-token verification does not take eight times AR decode.',figure='roofline',sources='roofline; results/ncu/*_ncu.json')
page('Memory-bound, compute-bound, latency-bound','BOTTLENECKS',[
 ('Memory-bound','A kernel spends its limiting time getting bytes from memory. Historical target decode and verify have about 95% of kernel time classified as memory-bound. Their dominant matrix operations reach high DRAM utilization while arithmetic units have substantial spare capacity. Low occupancy alone does not prove inefficiency when bandwidth is already saturated.'),
 ('Compute-bound','Arithmetic throughput is the limiting resource. Historical prefill has about 85% of kernel time classified compute-bound because its much larger matrix workload reuses weights across hundreds of tokens. Prefill and decode can therefore sit in different roofline regions for the same model.'),
 ('Latency-bound','Small operations may finish before the GPU fully utilizes memory or arithmetic capacity. Many short norm, attention, elementwise, KV and reduction kernels create overhead. The historical draft forward and tree step have about 29% and 37% latency-classified kernel time. This classification concerns active kernels; CPU gaps are additional.'),
 ('The practical lesson','A smaller draft model is not automatically fast enough to hide under target verification. Sequential depth repeats short operations and host work. Reducing rows in a memory/latency-limited kernel may barely shorten each step, while introducing reduced-row graph shapes can add overhead. Only complete-request timing answers whether the trade helps.')],
 'The limiting resource depends on phase and shape, not just model size or the use of tensor cores.',
 'Why can reducing a draft matrix from 24 rows to 8 fail to deliver a 3× reduction in step time?',sources='results/ncu/summary_tables.md; docs/perf_analysis.md')
page('Why synchronous drafting costs so much','HISTORICAL PHASE COSTS',[
 ('The serial SD round','The historical SD K6 trace spends roughly 24.9 ms drafting and 24.9 ms verifying per round. Drafting consists of sequential forwards. Speculation still accelerates AR by committing multiple tokens, but it pays drafting before target verification.'),
 ('The host gap','Each historical draft forward has about 3.49 ms wall duration but about 2.42 ms of GPU work. The roughly 1.1 ms difference includes host/launch and synchronization gaps. It is not an extra 1.1 ms CUDA kernel. Repeating that gap across serial draft steps makes it important.'),
 ('SSD’s different limit','SSD attempts to hide draft construction behind target verification. The historical glue phase is about 4.3 ms and tree decoding about 25.4 ms. Together they can outlast a roughly 24.9 ms verify phase. The target then waits despite the GPUs being separate.'),
 ('What changed later','Those durations describe the original historical execution path and three diagnosed prompts under profiling. The completed shared-backend performance campaign has different request times and a broader workload. The modern context profiles show K7 tree/verify spans near one and K10 near 1.6. Do not mix these numbers into a single synthetic latency breakdown.')],
 'SSD can hide work only if the work and its delivery become ready in time.',
 'Explain why “a tiny model must be cheap” is insufficient for a serial drafter.',sources='docs/perf_analysis.md; context profiles')
page('Why the original outputs diverged','NUMERICAL CORRECTNESS',[
 ('The mathematical algorithm is not the whole execution','Greedy decoding chooses the largest final score, called a logit. Finite-precision additions and reductions are not perfectly associative. Single-query and batched paths may use different matrix kernels, reduction orders or attention splits. Close logits can exchange order even with the same weights and visible input.'),
 ('A conceptual example','Imagine two candidate scores that differ by a tiny amount. One execution rounds candidate A upward and another rounds B upward. The next chosen token differs, so subsequent histories differ too. After that, comparing later logits without restoring matching history does not isolate the first numerical cause. This is an illustration, not an actual captured pair of scores.'),
 ('What our investigation established','We replayed matching prefixes, compared projection arithmetic across layers and checked attention around page/split boundaries. Some original single-query/batched attention cases differed. The original engine also exhibited output discrepancies; it was not safe to attribute everything to publication edits. Historical AR output drift was separately retained as a failed reproduction gate.'),
 ('Do not overstate the diagnosis','This is evidence about specific numerical execution differences. It is not a blanket claim that hardware randomly produces wrong answers, that every mismatch is harmless, or that a tolerance check satisfies token-for-token agreement. The original AR output requirement remains distinct from the chosen shared reference.')],
 'A small numerical difference can cause a large textual difference; diagnose the first divergence on the same history.',
 'Why are logits after the first differing output token a poor isolated correctness comparison?',sources='docs/shared_fast_backend.md; results/correctness matching-prefix and attention controls')
page('Which correctness tests establish what?','EVIDENCE BOUNDARIES',[
 ('Operation-level controls','The fixed-shape projection candidate matched its own one-query reference in 870/870 operation cases across real weights and matching prefixes. Attention tests covered widths 5, 7, 8 and 9 at nine prefix lengths: original batching matched individual queries in 26/36 cases; the shared attention path matched in 36/36. These are controlled component tests, not a proof for all generation.'),
 ('Whole-generation gates','The shared-backend gate had 36/36 paired speculative comparisons against shared AR. It did not pass the historical-original-AR requirement. Shared AR differed from original AR on seven of nine tested prompts under each protocol. Those are separate facts and cannot be combined into a generic “all correctness passed” label.'),
 ('Current material’s direct audit','For this package, we independently re-read valid receipts and compare output token IDs: 1,500 speculative baseline outputs, 2,250 publication outputs, 252 context-study outputs, and {depth_exact} completed unequal-depth test outputs match their declared shared references. These reuse prompt sets and are not additive evidence of that many independent unseen tasks.'),
 ('Engineering coverage','Partial-prefix, rejection, all-accepted/bonus, EOS, stale state, boundaries and draining require explicit coverage. The greedy handoff successor has CPU tests and a queued CUDA/full-model gate. Passing its host tests does not yet establish safe asynchronous GPU behavior.')],
 'Report the reference, coverage and level of each test; a component test and a full-model gate answer different questions.',
 'How can a gate have 36/36 paired matches while its overall historical-reference status is failed?',sources='shared_gate; docs/shared_fast_backend.md; analysis.json; greedy_validation')
page('What the shared backend actually changes','SHARED-FIXED16-V1',[
 ('Shared means the same target arithmetic','AR, SD and SSD use a common target projection shape and compatible attention computation. Some target operations pad to sixteen query rows, and attention evaluates each query with a consistent visible length and reduction structure. Weights remain BF16 Qwen3. The drafter’s role and verification algorithm remain recognizable.'),
 ('Why it helps agreement','AR’s one useful query and SD/SSD’s multi-query verification no longer switch the target’s numerical implementation merely because of query count. Identical committed history can therefore produce the same greedy target decision in the tested scope. The sixteen internal rows are padding, not sixteen concurrent requests.'),
 ('There is a cost','A separate three-prompt dedicated engineering benchmark measured original AR at 5.619 s and shared AR at 6.281 s, about 11.8% slower. Shared SD and SSD remained faster than shared AR there. That small diagnostic is not the 75-prompt campaign and cannot establish a universal overhead percentage.'),
 ('How to state the claim','“Our speculative variants match shared-fixed16-v1 AR on the tested workload.” Do not say they reproduce original AR’s words. This numerical infrastructure is not the project’s novel scheduling contribution. Model quality versus original AR has not been independently evaluated, and matching generated token IDs is not a semantic-quality benchmark.')],
 'The shared reference makes the experiment internally consistent while explicitly changing AR’s execution contract.',
 'Does unchanged model weight data imply unchanged numerical execution or identical generated words?',sources='shared_gate; numerical_benchmark; docs/shared_fast_backend.md')
page('The completed baseline result','BASELINE PERFORMANCE',[
 ('The controlled comparison','The campaign completes 480 tuning requests and 2,250 test requests: three methods × 75 prompts × five rounds × two output protocols. Scoring is unprofiled, warm, batch one and monitored on dedicated GPUs. SD selected K6 and SSD K7/F3 before test timing.'),
 ('Read the fixed-output bars','Mean request latency is {ar_fixed:.3f} s for shared AR, {sd_fixed:.3f} s for SD and {ssd_fixed:.3f} s for SSD. Their committed output rates are about 40.8, 81.0 and 115.9 tokens/s. SD is about 1.98× and SSD 2.84× faster than the shared AR baseline on this frozen mix.'),
 ('What the result supports','Speculation works on this executor and hardware. The SSD gain over AR includes use of a second GPU; it is not an equal-resource efficiency result. These are baseline algorithm gains. The project contribution must be judged by an additional comparison with the strongest complete-cache SSD setting.'),
 ('What it does not support','A speedup against a deliberately weaker K10 comparator does not show that our scheduler improves the best complete SSD. Nor do these measurements establish stochastic-sampling correctness, many-request serving throughput or generalization beyond the declared models, inputs and protocols.')],
 'The baseline establishes a strong platform and comparator; it is not proof that early publication is beneficial.',
 'Which bar should an early-publication optimization beat before claiming an incremental project speedup?',figure='baseline_fixed',sources='baseline; baseline_selection; analysis.json')
page('Why code, chat and documents differ','PER-FAMILY ANALYSIS',[
 ('The measured pattern','Fixed-output complete SSD averages about 1.434 s on code, 2.382 s on chat and 2.809 s on document prompts. AR is close to 6.24–6.33 s across these families because it produces one token per target step. Speculative performance depends strongly on how well draft predictions match the target.'),
 ('Acceptance explains part of it','SSD draft acceptance is about 0.629 on code, 0.363 on chat and 0.316 on document tasks in this sample. Outcome-cache hits are also higher on code, about 0.886 versus 0.754 and 0.691. More useful draft work and fewer misses allow more committed tokens per target round.'),
 ('Interpretation, not a universal ranking','This suggests acceptance and cache coverage matter at least as much as input length alone. The tasks differ in content and formatting as well as length, so the chart is not a controlled experiment attributing every difference to one variable. We have not measured whether generated code is more correct or documents better summarized.'),
 ('Why report family regressions','An aggregate speedup could hide a severe loss on one task type. The baseline has no mean-latency family regression against shared AR. Our policy comparisons still need their own family tables; a promising overall mean should not erase a document-task slowdown.')],
 'A balanced mean is useful, but acceptance and regressions must be inspected by family.',
 'What experiment would isolate input length from task content more directly?',figure='family_latency',sources='baseline per-family rows')
page('Natural EOS, first delivery and memory','SECONDARY RESULTS',[
 ('Natural stopping','Mean natural-EOS request latency is {ar_natural:.3f} s for AR, {sd_natural:.3f} s for SD and {ssd_natural:.3f} s for SSD. The mean output length is about 221.2 tokens; the cap remains 256. The same fixed-length-selected configurations are used. Matching outputs also means matching stopping behavior on these paired tests.'),
 ('First output can arrive later','The baseline’s mean fixed-output first delivery is about {ar_fixed_first_ms:.1f} ms for AR, {sd_fixed_first_ms:.1f} ms for SD and {ssd_fixed_first_ms:.1f} ms for SSD. Speculative methods can finish the request earlier while initially waiting for drafting or both-model preparation. This tradeoff matters for interactive use and should not be hidden behind total throughput.'),
 ('Memory has several meanings','In the fresh publication comparison, complete SSD peak target allocation is about 35.1 GiB and draft allocation 31.1 GiB, including preallocated KV capacity. Snapshot buffers and score history can add storage even when a tree has fewer live rows. Report actual peaks alongside configured fractions; do not equate reserved memory with actively useful state.'),
 ('Keep tables comparable','Natural EOS is secondary. Report output lengths, p50/p95, first delivery, acceptance and GPU counts alongside mean latency. A different set of shorter answers is not a clean acceleration result. Our numerical reference remains the shared target implementation.')],
 'Total latency, first delivery, memory and output length describe different user and resource tradeoffs.',
 'Why might a lower natural-EOS mean coexist with nearly unchanged p50?',figure='baseline_natural',sources='baseline; publication statistics')
page('The first uniform publication experiment','UNIFORM PUBLICATION',[
 ('The minimum depth is fixed','Uniform publication means the same minimum P throughout a run. The drafter may return more than P tokens if more are complete. The first study held K7/F3 fixed and tried P1, P2, P4 and P7 on 24 tuning prompts with two repetitions.'),
 ('What it selected','The best positive-P setting was P7. Since P equals K, the selected setting did not hand over a partial continuation. The 1,500-request comparison found complete SSD at about 2.2006 s and publication at 2.2230 s for fixed256, roughly a 1% slowdown. Natural-EOS means were about 1.9045 s and 1.9203 s.'),
 ('Why this matters','The experiment does not merely say P4 was unlucky on one prompt: it tunes multiple minimum depths and finds that the completed behavior wins among those positive-P candidates. The publication machinery still incurs checks and handling. Lower P can trade less waiting for fewer accepted tokens and more verification rounds.'),
 ('A different engineering diagnostic','K10 checks did exercise partial handoffs. Those timing observations included small boundary-oriented inputs and diagnostic allocations, so they are not headline evidence against the strongest baseline. The later dedicated publication comparison supplies the stronger result.')],
 'P7 winning at K7 is evidence that the selected “early” policy behaved like complete publication.',
 'Why does a minimum of four not imply every delivered continuation has length four?',sources='uniform; uniform_tune; docs/adaptive_publication.md')
page('Bounded checkpoints and the adaptive rule','EARLIER PROTOTYPES',[
 ('Bounded checkpointing','The host records completion events and checks them without blocking, while allowing at most two unfinished draft steps. It can submit efficiently instead of synchronizing after every eligible step. When handing over state, this prototype still drains already-submitted work before exposure.'),
 ('A local adaptive heuristic','The target shares recent verify time, accepted counts and rejection information. A request-local rule estimates committed work per cost for candidate depths and continues only if its estimated cost improves by more than 5%. It uses a smoothed acceptance model and sparse-history fallback, not future target outcomes.'),
 ('What the diagnostic found','The gate completed 16 blocks and 96 requests; 84 speculative outputs matched fresh shared AR. It exercised partial handoffs. The heuristic did not choose additional depth in the completed diagnostic, so that run does not demonstrate useful adaptive decisions or better latency. Performance_evidence is explicitly false.'),
 ('Why this is not the final scheduler','The rule approximates per-token survival and does not fully model misses, transfer or future-round effects. It is separate from the static rank-depth prototype and is not combined with overlap in the queued unequal-depth study. “Adaptive is implemented” must not be translated into “the proposal’s complete outcome-aware scheduler is finished”.')],
 'A causal decision rule can be implemented correctly without having evidence that its decisions help.',
 'Which missing cost terms could make a simple accepted-tokens/time heuristic choose poorly?',sources='adaptive; docs/adaptive_publication.md')
page('What stable-prefix overlap changes','OVERLAP IMPLEMENTATION',[
 ('The difference at handoff','Uniform/bounded paths finish submitted work before exposing the continuation. The overlap path selects a completed eligible prefix, copies its valid data to independent storage on a handoff stream, and sends that snapshot while previously queued tail work may still run.'),
 ('What remains serialized','The producer must join the response and every submitted tail write before canonical graph buffers or KV state are reused. Cache misses also drain old work before JIT drafting. Thus overlap moves a dependency out of handoff; it does not delete work or preempt kernels.'),
 ('Actual overlap was observed','The normal K10 engineering gate recorded 633 responses before tail completion across both protocols. An artificial-delay stress gate also exercised the mechanism. All 102 speculative gate/stress comparisons matched fresh shared AR. These low-KV, single-repetition diagnostics include recorded shared-GPU activity and cannot rank performance.'),
 ('The host limitation','The existing overlap path checks requests on the draft-submission host thread. A request arriving after full-snapshot selection may still wait. K7 had no measured response-before-tail handoffs in those inputs. This motivates independent service and finer request/readiness instrumentation, not a claim that the opportunity is definitely absent.')],
 'A separate stream creates an opportunity; response-before-tail counters establish actual overlap.',
 'Why must the producer still drain after it has already sent the snapshot?',figure='handoff_schematic',sources='overlap_gate; overlap_stress; docs/prefix_publication.md')
page('The dedicated publication comparison','INITIAL PROJECT RESULT',[
 ('A stronger experiment','Fresh complete, uniform and overlap SSD arms were tuned and then compared on the same 75 reused test prompts, five rounds, under both protocols. There are 1,008 tuning and 2,250 test requests, plus three scored AR continuity checks. No profiler is attached; both GPUs are dedicated.'),
 ('The frozen winners','Complete selected K7; uniform selected K7/P7; overlap selected K7/P4/W2. Fixed-output means are {complete_pub:.4f}, {uniform_pub:.4f} and {overlap_pub:.4f} s. Both publication variants are about 1% slower. Their confidence intervals support a measured regression against complete K7 on this design.'),
 ('What the execution counters say','At K7 the delivered cached continuations all have length seven and there are zero response-before-tail events. Accepted work and cache behavior do not provide an offsetting gain. Checks, synchronization and handling add cost without a demonstrated useful early handoff in the selected configuration.'),
 ('Keep the interpretation bounded','The result does not prove that no implementation could exploit readiness. It establishes that these versions and selected settings did not improve the strongest fresh comparator. Original AR is not rerun as a full arm here: its saved shared-reference timings are historical context, while SSD-versus-SSD comparisons are fresh and paired.')],
 'The main project performance result is a quantified regression, with a clear execution explanation and next diagnostic.',
 'What does the confidence interval say, and what does the zero-overlap counter not prove?',figure='publication_effect',sources='publication; publication_selection; analysis.json')
page('Did longer contexts reveal an advantage?','CONTEXT LENGTH',[
 ('The controlled setup','One synthetic template per family is extended with repeated irrelevant notes to exactly 256, 512, 1,024 or 2,048 input tokens. Seven methods/settings run three rounds, fixed256 output. This creates 252 unprofiled requests. Twelve separate profiling blocks add 36 diagnostic requests. It is not a large unseen long-document benchmark.'),
 ('What improves','Overlap K10 is about 11.8–23.4% faster than complete K10 across the four lengths. At 2,048 input tokens, complete K10 takes 3.655 s and overlap K10 takes 2.896 s. Uniform K10/P4 takes 2.853 s and is also a strong simple partial-handoff control.'),
 ('What still wins','Complete K7 takes 2.668 s at 2,048 input tokens and remains faster at every measured length. Against that baseline, overlap K10 is still about 7–16% slower across the sweep. Publication K7 adds about 1% and records no response-before-tail handoffs.'),
 ('No extrapolated crossover','The relative benefit versus K10 is not monotonic with context length, and the four measured points do not establish a future crossover with K7. Content and acceptance vary with the synthetic construction. Longer output lengths, natural long inputs and capacity beyond 4,096 are distinct future tests.')],
 'There is a same-K10 benefit, but no advantage over the strongest K7 comparator at the measured lengths.',
 'At 2,048 tokens, which comparison supports an improvement and which comparison rejects a broad win?',figure='context_latency',sources='context')
page('Does drafting scale differently from verify?','CONTEXT PHASE ANALYSIS',[
 ('Read the ratio','Separate profiles estimate the full tree’s device span divided by target verification device span. Complete K7 is near one at all four input lengths. Complete K10 is about 1.56–1.64. A ratio above one suggests the full draft tree tends to outlast the target’s verification window.'),
 ('Input length had a modest effect here','K7 full-tree spans are roughly 27–28 ms and verify spans roughly 27–28 ms. K10 full-tree spans are roughly 44–46 ms while verify remains about 27–28 ms. Increasing the initial context from 256 to 2,048 did not radically change this balance on the measured executor.'),
 ('Acceptance and waiting still change','The unprofiled K7 request grows from about 1.791 s to 2.668 s. Tokens per verification and prompt construction differ, and request prefill also grows. Request scaling cannot be inferred solely by multiplying one isolated verify duration. At 2,048 tokens, K10 overlap reduces wait versus complete K10, but commits fewer tokens per verify and still loses to K7.'),
 ('Limits of span measurement','These are profiled spans with launch gaps, not isolated kernel-cost sums. Partial or uncorrelated trees are not extrapolated to full K-step cost. Input length is the starting context; generated tokens increase the actual attention history during the request.')],
 'Draft-versus-verify balance is informative, but acceptance, prefill and handoff decide total latency.',
 'Why does a 1.6 tree/verify ratio not imply a guaranteed 1.6× publication speedup?',figure='phase_balance',sources='context profiles and unprofiled rows')
page('Different outcomes now get different depths','UNEQUAL-DEPTH IMPLEMENTATION',[
 ('What is implemented','Recovery candidates keep the original ranking and F3 fan-out at each acceptance position. The highest-ranked remaining token gets more continuation depth: K7 uses [7,4,4], K10 uses [10,7,4]. The same rule applies at each acceptance position; rank is only a likelihood proxy.'),
 ('Actual work is removed','Deeper forwards exclude retired rows instead of computing and masking them. Compact attention masks preserve branch ancestry; valid lengths exclude uncomputed tails. Target K+1 verification is unchanged.'),
 ('The controls','Tuned complete and overlap SSD are practical controls. Reversed priority tests ranking; uniform equal-work depth tests allocation against a simple budget. Equal rows do not match every execution cost.'),
 ('What is not implemented','This v1 is static: no learned outcome frequencies or global expected-benefit scheduler. Reserved KV and rectangular score/snapshot memory are unchanged. Fewer rows alone are a weak success criterion.')],
 'This is a real compacted unequal-depth prototype, with a deliberately limited static policy.',
 'What additional observations would a cost-aware outcome scheduler need beyond top-token rank?',figure='depth_allocation',sources='.worktrees/outcome-depth-v1/docs/outcome_depth.md; depth_selection')
page('The unequal-depth results so far','PROVISIONAL RESULT',[
 ('What has completed','The correctness gate completes 28 blocks and 168 requests; all 156 speculative comparisons match fresh shared AR. Tuning completes 26 blocks and 624 requests. Test execution has {depth_blocks}/60 valid blocks and {depth_exact} scored outputs, all matching saved shared AR at the frozen cutoff.'),
 ('Compare only complete paired rounds','The fixed-output plot uses three rounds with all six methods complete: 225 requests per method. Natural EOS uses four complete rounds: 300 per method. Other valid blocks contribute to the progress count but not an unbalanced comparative mean. Final five-round conclusions must wait.'),
 ('The measured direction','Ranked K7 is about 3.7% slower than complete SSD under fixed output and about 4.0% slower under natural EOS. Ranked+overlap is also slower. Reversed priority is worse still, about 15–16% slower. The ranked tree computes 28.6% fewer query rows, yet overall latency increases.'),
 ('What we learned','Ranking seems less harmful than reversing priorities in this sample, but neither that observation nor reduced rows establishes acceleration. Shorter continuation availability, more target rounds, memory/launch-limited kernels and policy overhead are candidate mechanisms. Detailed traces are needed before assigning an exact causal share to each.')],
 'Use “provisional slowdown with reduced work,” not “completed optimization success.”',
 'Why do {depth_blocks} valid blocks not justify averaging every available measurement across all six methods?',figure='unequal_depth',sources='depth_gate; depth_selection; analysis.json')
page('The queued greedy handoff successor','IMPLEMENTED, GPU UNTESTED',[
 ('Preserve the comparator','The main-checkout complete SSD implementation remains unchanged. The successor lives on a separate branch and imports the original checkout for its legacy baseline arms. It keeps the shared target arithmetic and verification graph. Performance workers have no artificial delays or diagnostic tracing.'),
 ('Four changes to diagnose and test','First, record prefix readiness, request posting, host observation, copying, target receipt and full completion with clock uncertainty. Second, cache/transfer token IDs without full draft score history for greedy verification. The vocabulary head still runs to select tokens. Third, a separate CPU service thread responds from completed storage while only the producer runs models or writes KV. Fourth, an immutable complete-ready view bypasses a snapshot copy.'),
 ('Six controlled arms','Legacy complete and legacy overlap preserve the baseline. IDs-only complete isolates transport/cache savings. IDs-only inline publication isolates publication with cheaper payloads. IDs-only threaded service isolates host independence. IDs-only threaded+bypass measures the combined version. K is tuned per arm before the repeated comparison.'),
 ('Current validation boundary','The recorded CPU suite passed 253 tests with 19 skips before queuing. CUDA ownership tests, stress coverage and full-model output checks run before performance. At this snapshot no GPU block has run. We cannot report a new speedup or claim the new asynchronous GPU path is validated.')],
 'The new version targets a measured execution question; its benefit and GPU correctness remain unmeasured.',
 'Why must IDs-only complete be included alongside the optimized publication arm?',sources='greedy_validation; greedy_status; docs/greedy_handoff_queue.md')
page('Experiment ledger: completed is not queued','EVIDENCE INVENTORY',[
 ('Use the right evidence tier','The table separates completed timing studies from engineering diagnostics and partial execution. Each study has its own numerical contract and workload. Adding all request counts would double-count reused prompts and mix distinct evidence levels.'),
 ('Failures remain visible','Early setup failures, contaminated attempts and superseded queue versions are retained but excluded from accepted measurements. The publication/context v1 queues were superseded when redundant AR timing was removed. Their directories are not additional independent completed campaigns.'),
 ('Why current queues wait','Dedicated performance requires both UUID-pinned GPUs free of unrelated compute. Completed blocks survive. The unequal-depth watcher resumes the remaining 17 blocks before the greedy successor proceeds. Its waiting state is evidence of execution status, not a result about the algorithm.')],
 'Use completed performance, diagnostic correctness, partial results and queued work as distinct labels.',
 'Which studies can support a headline latency comparison today?',table=[['Study','Evidence at frozen cutoff'],['Shared baselines','Complete: 480 tune + 2,250 test'],['First uniform comparison','Complete: 1,500 test requests'],['Bounded/adaptive and overlap gates','Correctness/mechanism diagnostics'],['Fresh publication v2','Complete: 1,008 tune + 2,250 test'],['Context sweep v2','Complete: 252 unprofiled + 36 profiled'],['Unequal depths v2','Gate+tune complete; {depth_blocks}/60 test blocks'],['Greedy successor','Implemented; GPU validation queued']],sources='evidence_manifest.json; block_audit.json; source summaries')
page('What remains, and why it matters','REMAINING PLAN',[
 ('Finish the current comparison','Complete the remaining unequal-depth test blocks and publish the final paired five-round report. This resolves repeatability, per-family regressions and uncertainty for the static depth allocation, rather than adding another unbalanced partial comparison.'),
 ('Test the cheaper independent handoff','Run CUDA ownership and full-model correctness gates first. The six-arm campaign then isolates transport, service thread and full-ready bypass. Timestamp diagnostics ask whether the useful window was lost before the request, during host observation, while copying, or during delivery. A failed gate stops performance claims.'),
 ('Only pursue complexity with evidence','If usable prefix readiness exists but service cost consumes it, improve service. If smaller prefixes create too many full K+1 verifications, prototype shorter verification buckets and revalidate exact outputs. Padding to sixteen rows means fewer nominal tokens may save little computation.'),
 ('Proposal work still unestablished','A learned or measured-cost outcome scheduler must outperform uniform and static rank budgets, including overhead and fallback. Broader unseen prompts, natural long contexts, output-cap sensitivity and RTX 3090 replication remain separate studies. An 8K study requires changed capacity/memory checks. Online global-depth/fan-out and timing-rule adaptations are proposal comparisons, not completed implementations.'),
 ('A defensible final outcome','The proposal allows a quantified negative result if advanced scheduling lacks headroom. Preserve the simpler correct baseline, explain the bottleneck and report where partial publication helps a matched oversized K but fails to beat tuned K7.')],
 'Every next experiment should resolve a named uncertainty; the 10% improvement remains an engineering target.',
 'If the new transport improves both complete and publication SSD equally, what incremental publication claim remains?',sources='Proposal pp. 1–2; docs/greedy_handoff_queue.md; analysis.json')
page('Glossary and evidence map','QUICK REFERENCE',[
 ('Core vocabulary','AR: target-only autoregressive generation. SD: sequential draft then target verification. SSD: prepare likely next continuations concurrently. Prefill: process the input history. KV cache: stored attention keys/values. Outcome cache: prepared continuations for possible target outcomes. JIT: draft on demand after a cache miss.'),
 ('Execution vocabulary','Kernel: device program. Stream: ordered device command queue. Event: recorded completion/dependency marker. CUDA graph: captured execution replay. Snapshot: independently owned completed handoff data. Drain: wait for submitted accesses before retirement. Epoch/generation: identity used to reject stale state.'),
 ('Measurement vocabulary','Latency: elapsed interval for one request. p50/p95: empirical request percentiles. Acceptance: accepted/verified draft tokens. First delivery: first output callback interval. Throughput here: committed tokens divided by request seconds. CI: bootstrap uncertainty interval. FLOP/B: arithmetic operations per DRAM byte. NCCL: GPU communication library.'),
 ('Evidence you can inspect','evidence_manifest.json lists the cutoff and source hashes. analysis.json stores the direct output audit and paired partial results. figure_data.json and figure_values.csv store plotted values. block_audit.json lists accepted receipts. The evidence archive preserves raw results and executed source copies. The builders read saved inputs and never start GPU work.'),
 ('Your final rehearsal','Open the presentation PDF, keep the speaker guide closed and explain each plot in four sentences: what was compared, what was held fixed, what changed, and what remains unknown. Then answer the following twenty-five questions. Keep a concise answer ready before expanding technically.')],
 'When challenged, name the reference, the measurement interval, and the evidence limitation.',
 'Find the source for one plotted point and reproduce its latency-change percentage.',sources='evidence_manifest.json; analysis.json; figure_manifest.json')

QA=[
 ('What is our research contribution?','Safe incremental publication and a compacted unequal-depth outcome-cache prototype, plus reproducible evidence of when they help or add overhead. SSD itself and batch-independent arithmetic are prior ideas; their baseline speedups are not our new scheduling result.','Implementation / contribution'),
 ('Have we beaten tuned complete SSD?','No. The completed publication comparison is about 1% slower than complete K7. Partial unequal-depth results are also slower. K10 publication beats complete K10 in the context diagnostic, but not the stronger complete K7 comparator.','Results / honesty'),
 ('What does P=4 actually mean?','At least four continuation tokens must be safely complete before that path is eligible to publish. It may deliver more. P is fixed in uniform publication and does not refer to a neural-network layer or a number of user requests.','Implementation'),
 ('How do uniform and overlap handoff differ?','The earlier path finishes already-submitted work before exposure. Overlap copies a completed prefix into independent storage and can deliver it while the submitted tail finishes. It still joins outstanding accesses before KV or graph-buffer reuse.','Implementation / tradeoff'),
 ('What has not run in the greedy successor?','GPU ownership tests, stress coverage, full-model correctness gates and performance measurements are queued. CPU validation exists, but it is insufficient to establish correctness of the asynchronous GPU lifetime behavior or a latency improvement.','Remaining work'),
 ('What is F and what is unequal depth?','F is the recovery-candidate fan-out per possible acceptance position. F3 gives three candidate ranks. Unequal depth assigns those ranks different continuation budgets, such as [7,4,4], instead of preparing seven tokens for every rank.','Implementation'),
 ('Is the unequal-depth scheduler adaptive?','No. Its v1 budgets depend on static recovery rank and are identical across acceptance positions. A separate local adaptive publication heuristic exists, but a learned/cost-aware allocation policy from the proposal remains future work.','Implementation / limitations'),
 ('Why are fewer rows not automatically faster?','Small draft forwards can be memory- or launch-limited, so shrinking rows saves little time per step. Shorter continuations may increase target verification rounds. Policy handling and draining also cost time. Query-row reduction measures work, not full-request latency.','Analysis'),
 ('Why include reversed and equal-work controls?','Reversal tests whether prioritizing higher-ranked candidates matters. Uniform equal-work depth tests whether the uneven allocation beats a simpler matching query-row budget. Those controls do not match every kernel, padding or memory-traffic cost.','Ablations'),
 ('Why can target verification be memory-bound?','At small query width, streaming the large target weights dominates. Several query rows reuse those weights without proportionally increasing elapsed time. Historical Nsight Compute counters place verification left of the measured roofline ridge, with high memory utilization.','Profiling'),
 ('What does Nsight Systems capture?','A correlated CPU/GPU timeline: host ranges, CUDA API submissions, kernels, streams, copies and communication. Our NVTX labels connect these events to prefill, drafting, verification and handoff. It reveals where time and dependencies occur.','Profiling'),
 ('What does Nsight Compute add?','Hardware-counter analysis of selected kernels or phases: arithmetic intensity, DRAM traffic, computation/memory utilization, occupancy and stalls. Our five historical captures classify decode, verify, draft, tree step and prefill. Profiling is kept separate from headline timing.','Profiling'),
 ('Does 92% GPU busy mean good utilization?','Not necessarily. In the historical target trace, about 35% of the full window is NCCL kernel time, which can include waiting for peer data. Kernel activity is different from useful model progress or arithmetic-unit utilization.','Profiling'),
 ('How much of 9.2 ms is actual transfer?','We have no isolated exact transfer share. About 8.0 ms aligns with unfinished-tree completion in the historical trace, but the residual also contains host handling and synchronization. The queued correlated timestamp diagnostic is designed to locate the remaining opportunity.','Analysis / uncertainty'),
 ('Does a separate CUDA stream prove overlap?','No. Dependencies and GPU resource constraints can serialize it. We record responses completed before the submitted tail event finishes. Actual event coverage establishes overlap; only full-request comparisons establish whether it improves latency.','Implementation / evidence'),
 ('Why did original AR and speculation disagree?','Different execution shapes selected different finite-precision arithmetic/reduction paths. Close target logits could change order and flip a greedy token. Matching-prefix projection and attention controls supplied evidence. Later text diverges because the histories then differ.','Correctness'),
 ('What does the shared numerical reference fix?','It gives AR and speculative verification consistent target arithmetic for the tested scope. Outputs match shared-fixed16-v1 AR. It does not guarantee original AR wording: shared AR differed on seven of nine diagnosed prompts, and historical reproduction remained a separate failed gate.','Correctness / limits'),
 ('Are sixteen padded rows a batch of sixteen requests?','No. They are an internal shape used for consistent target operations. The request batch size remains one. Verification rows, outcome-tree rows, continuation depth and simultaneous user requests are distinct dimensions.','Technical clarity'),
 ('Why are AR and SD on one GPU but SSD on two?','That is the declared implementation allocation: AR target-only, SD both models on GPU0, SSD target GPU0 and draft GPU1. The result is a latency comparison with additional SSD hardware, not a claim of identical resource efficiency.','Experimental design'),
 ('Why reuse AR rather than rerun every campaign?','Saved exact output references are hash-validated against the model, inputs, numerical contract and dedicated receipts, with short fresh continuity checks. Historical timings stay labeled separately. Fresh SSD-versus-SSD arms provide the primary policy comparison.','Experimental design'),
 ('What is the point of two output protocols?','Fixed256 controls output work by ignoring EOS. Natural EOS measures variable response lengths up to the same cap. Both use winners selected on fixed-length tuning. Reporting lengths and agreement prevents confusing shorter output with faster execution.','Experimental design'),
 ('What does five repetitions establish?','It estimates repeated-run variation and output repeatability on the same prompts. It does not create new tasks. Our hierarchical paired bootstrap preserves round/prompt grouping and family balance rather than treating 375 measurements as unrelated prompts.','Statistics'),
 ('Did longer contexts help our method?','They exposed a useful K10 partial-handoff regime, but complete K7 remained faster at every measured input length. The trend was not monotonic, and the synthetic three-template sweep does not establish unseen long-document generalization.','Results / interpretation'),
 ('Why not claim the CPU-tested successor works already?','The CPU tests cannot exercise actual cross-stream writes, consumer lifetime and full-model output behavior. Its queued CUDA and generation gates must pass before performance, and every optimized arm must be compared with unchanged legacy complete SSD.','Validation'),
 ('What would change your conclusion next?','A fully validated version with lower full-request latency than tuned complete SSD, supported by paired repeated results and disclosed family regressions. Timestamp evidence should explain the mechanism; a stronger unseen workload should test whether the effect generalizes.','Remaining plan')]
for group in range(5):
 items=QA[group*5:(group+1)*5]
 page(f'Questions you should be ready to answer · {group+1}/5','REHEARSAL',[(f'{group*5+i+1}. {q}',a) for i,(q,a,r) in enumerate(items)],'Give the short answer first; add a measurement, comparator and limitation when challenged.','Close the guide and answer these five aloud. Then check your wording against the evidence.',sources='Answers summarize the cited implementation docs and frozen results on pp. 3–39.')
assert len(PAGES)==45
